race day your pulse pounds your head  throbs your adrenalin rushes the  starting gun goes off and so do you into  a wall of creeping runners that  accordion leg crush of runners waiting  to get out of their starting Corral's  intrigued scientists who wondered if the  movement could be modeled to find out  they filmed thousands of runners from  high level balconies as they left the  starting Corral's of several major  marathons scientists used an algorithm  to track the speed of runners as they  move to the starting line when directed  by race staffers runners shuffled  forward several meters before stopping  and starting up again the speed and  density changed and moved backward  through the pack of runners like a wave  the scientists discovered that the waves  move back at a constant rate which means  that they can estimate how long it takes  for the change in speed to travel all  the way through the crowd the faster  blocks were usually the same length as  the distance the race staffers moved  forward each time they set out from a  complete stop but unlike the waves that  traveled back through the racers no  waves traveled from side to side  despite imperfections in the crowds  movement this is one of the first  studies to look at the physics of human  movement not at the individual level but  at the level of the whole crowd similar  to previous studies in bacteria  scientists say this research could be  helpful for crowd management or creating  more accurate visual effects of crowds  and movies their model of how waves of  speed and density traveled they're  confined crowds could also lay the  foundation for understanding how crowds  respond to emergencies for now it could  help make your cue up to the starting  line a little bit smoother  [Music]
 

